{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>A man who rebukes will afterward find more favor than one who smooths the tongue (Proverbs 28:23).</b>" "<b>Shlomo</b> the king, peace be upon him, used to rebuke all the people of the world, and he would warn everyone who was fit to rebuke that he should rebuke after him and fill his place, and from him they would see and so do. And because the one rebuked is inclined toward the traits of the ground (adamah), when he does a thing that requires rebuke, therefore he said 'one who rebukes a man (adam)' and did not say 'one who rebukes a person (ish).' Know that most rebukes are directed at the masses of the people, and the masses of the people — their opinions differ. If their opinions were aligned and equal and matched with one another, they would be called individuals, not a mass; and since their opinions diverge and differ one from another, they do not accept rebuke, for the thing loved by this one is hated by that one, and what pleases this one will harm that one. So too they said, of blessed memory: said Rabbi Tarfon, I wonder if there is anyone in this generation who accepts rebuke. One says to him, 'Remove the splinter from between your teeth' — he replies, 'Remove the beam from between your eyes.' And each and every one says to the rebuker, 'Your tallit is purer than mine.' Therefore the rebuker is not accepted and not pleasing to all of them, for some of them hate him because he makes known to them the truth about their deeds, while the one who smooths his tongue for them is accepted and pleasing in their eyes and finds favor with them, as they said, of blessed memory: 'This scholar whom the townspeople love, because he does not rebuke them in matters of heaven.' Therefore Shlomo, peace be upon him, said here, מוכיח אדם אחרי חן ימצא ממחליק לשון — he means: the rebuker who comes after me and grasps my ways, when he speaks rebuke harshly, even though he does not find favor in the eyes of the one rebuked, he will find favor in the eyes of HaShem, may He be exalted, when the rebuke is accepted; for words of rebuke, when they are harsh, are what bring a person to the success of the soul, which is the attainment of favor in the eyes of HaShem, may He be exalted, when he accepts the rebuke. And if the rebuker smooths his tongue to the one rebuked and validates his evil words and deeds — behold, he strengthens his hands, and the one rebuked does not find favor in this, for he will lose his soul. And so we find in Israel that they were rebuked by Moshe Rabbeinu, peace be upon him, and they found favor before the Holy One, blessed be He, because of the abundance of Moshe's rebukes, for he would rebuke them constantly with harsh words, as we find at the waters of Meribat Kadesh, where he said to them (Numbers 20): שמעו נא המורים, and likewise regarding the matter of the manna, where he said to them (Exodus 16): ונחנו מה כי תלינו עלינו." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> מוכיח אדם אחרי חן ימצא ממחליק לשון — 'one who rebukes' (מוכיח), this is Moshe. 'A man' (אדם), these are Israel, as it is said (Ezekiel 34): ואתן צאני צאן מרעיתי אדם אתם. 'Will afterward find' (אחרי), he rebuked them to bring them after him. 'Favor' (חן ימצא), therefore — as it is said (Exodus 33): כי מצאת חן בעיני. 'Than one who smooths the tongue' (ממחליק לשון), this is Bilam, who smoothed with his tongue toward Israel with blessings that he heaped upon them, and their hearts were lifted up and they fell at Shittim, twenty-four thousand. And of this it is said (Proverbs 27): נאמנים פצעי אוהב ונעתרות נשיקות שונא. For all that Israel would increase in sin, Moshe would increase in rebuking them and praying for them, until they were appeased before the Holy One, blessed be He, and found favor in His eyes. And so we find further that he rebuked them beyond the Jordan and spoke to them harshly, and he would roll into his words some of the transgressions they had already committed, and he would mention to them by name the places in which they had sinned against the Holy One, blessed be He — and they are the plains of Moab, at Shittim, and at the Sea of Reeds, and in the wilderness of Paran, and the matter of the calf, and similar things. And this is what is written:" "<b>These are the words which Moshe spoke to all Israel beyond the Jordan, in the wilderness, in the Arabah, opposite Suph, between Paran and between Tophel and Lavan, and Hatzerot, and Di Zahav.</b> 'In the wilderness' (במדבר) — he rebuked them for what they did in the wilderness, as it is said (Psalms 78:40): כמה ימרוהו במדבר יעציבוהו בישימון. 'In the Arabah' (בערבה) — Rashi explained: for the Arabah where they sinned with Baal Peor at Shittim in the plains of Moab. 'Opposite Suph' (מול סוף) — for what they said at the Sea of Reeds (Exodus 14:11): המבלי אין קברים במצרים, and likewise when they journeyed from the Sea of Reeds, as it is said (Psalms 106:7): וימרו על ים בים סוף. 'Between Paran' (בין פארן) — for what they did at Paran through the spies. 'And between Tophel and Lavan' (ובין תפל ולבן) — he rebuked them for the worthless words (דברי תפלות) they uttered about the manna, which was white (לבן), when they said (Numbers 21:5): ונפשנו קצה בלחם הקלוקל. 'And Hatzerot' (וחצרות) — this is the dispute of Korah and the slander of Miriam. 'And Di Zahav' (ודי זהב) — he rebuked them for the calf they made for themselves because of the abundance of gold (די זהב) they had, as it is said (Hosea 2:10–11): וכסף הרביתי להם וזהב עשו לבעל, end quote." "<b>And in <i>Eileh HaDevarim</i> Rabbah</b> they expounded: 'ודי זהב' — what is 'ודי זהב'? They said: Is He able to create a world in six days and spread it to its four winds and give us the Ten Commandments? This is 'ודי זהב' (ד״י = enough; זהב = gold — enough gold they had to make the calf). End quote. Behold, these words are all rebukes of Moshe for the sins of Israel, for they are not names of places in the wilderness, for they had already left the wilderness and entered the plains of Moab; nor do we find Tophel and Lavan and Di Zahav among the stations of the journeying. If so, all of it is said concerning matters of transgression and provocations with which Israel provoked Him in the wilderness in times past. And this is the opinion of Onkelos, who interpreted the sense of the words accordingly, for Moshe concealed his words and for the honor of Israel he mentioned them only in allusion." "<b>And because</b> this book is the fifth and last of all the five books of the Torah, therefore I wished to enlighten you here regarding the arrangement of the five books of the Torah — why they were set one after another in the order in which they are arranged in the Torah. And it is a well-known matter that although the books are five in number, with respect to the matter alluded to and hinted at in the book of Genesis, all is one bond and one structure. And the reason it began first with the book of Genesis is that the creation of the world is the root of faith, and from faith in creation, providence will be clarified, and from providence, punishment and reward will become clear; and since these matters are the foundations of the Torah, therefore the book of Genesis was devoted to the creation of the world and to providence over man, into whose hand were entrusted positive commandments and negative commandments — and regarding reward, when He placed him in the Garden of Eden, and regarding punishment, when He expelled him from there. Also the story of the Flood is a great sign and clear testimony to creation, to providence, and to punishment and reward, for Noah the righteous and his sons were saved on account of their merit, and the men of that generation were destroyed for their punishment. And after the book of Genesis, 'And these are the names' (ואלה שמות) was set apart, in which is the account of the patriarchs Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, who held fast to the faith and were the root of the unity; and therefore Genesis and Exodus were joined together, for faith in creation brings a person to faith in the unity. And after it, the book of Leviticus, which is the account of the sacrifices — and it is known that the essence of the unity is the sacrifices, for they bring the powers near to one another, unto the First Cause, may He be exalted, for the righteous person, by means of the sacrifice, unifies himself with that One who creates, may He be exalted. And after the book of Leviticus, the book of 'In the wilderness of Sinai' (Numbers) was set apart, in which is the account concerning the land of Israel; and these were joined to one another to make clear that the sacrifices are only in the land of Israel, for as soon as Israel received the Torah at Sinai they were worthy to enter the land, and had it not been for the sin of the spies, which delayed them in the wilderness forty years. And after the book of 'In the wilderness of Sinai,' this book, which is 'These are the words' (Deuteronomy), was set apart; and since the essential settlement of Israel in the land of Israel was not in the two Temples that passed, but only in the final redemption after which there is no exile, therefore He wished to conclude with this book, which speaks at its end of the final redemption, which is like the matter of the creation of the world and is the purpose of the creation of the world — in the manner of the book of Genesis with which He began, according to the principle of 'their end is fastened in their beginning,' tying all five books of the Torah one to another. For creation is the cause of the unity, and the unity is through the sacrifices, and the sacrifices have their essence only in the land of Israel, and Israel have no perfection at all times except in the final redemption after which there is no exile." "<b>And you should reflect</b> further on why this book begins with the word אלה ('these') and does not say ואלה הדברים ('and these are the words'), since all is bound together by a ו׳ ('and'). But this indicates that this fifth book, although it is joined with the four preceding ones and all is one bond and one structure, is nevertheless a matter unto itself — like the matter of the etrog, which is joined with its kindred species, for its commandment is only with them, yet it stands on its own in that it is not bound together with their binding. And so too the second ה׳ in the Name, which is the attribute of judgment, delivered the discipline to Israel on account of seven transgressions; and therefore this fifth book is called משנה תורה ('a repetition of the Torah'). Therefore Moses began with words of reproof, to speak harshly with Israel, mixing in gentle words among them. And so he reminded them of seven transgressions, and these are they: the complaints in the wilderness, and the sin of Peor, and their rebellion at the Sea of Reeds, and the sin of the spies, and the slander concerning the manna, and the discord of Korah, and the sin of the calf. Now תפל and לבן are one thing, and therefore they are seven, corresponding to what is written (Leviticus 26): ״ויספתי ליסרה אתכם שבע על חטאתיכם״ ('And I will add to chastise you seven times for your sins'). And for this reason Moses regularly mentions in this book ה׳ אלהינו ה׳ אלהיך ('YHWH our Elohim, YHWH your Elohim') — something you will not find in the preceding books; and the intent concerns the attribute of judgment, and this is the father of them all (Deuteronomy 4): ״כי ה׳ אלהיך אש אוכלה הוא״ — and he said אש אוכלה ('a consuming fire'), that is, from what you saw at the head of the mountain. And understand this."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:04.104651956Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}